# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source assets (Figma screenshot exports — not needed for the deploy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
scren/
screens/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated portfolio screenshots.
#
# scripts/capture.mjs and scripts/render-icon.mjs write into this directory.
# Nothing in app/, components/, lib/ or next.config.ts reads from it, so the
# output is regenerable and stays out of git. The eight shots embedded in
# README.md are the exception and ARE tracked.
#
# The numbered set replaced 127 files (50 MB) — a per-section sweep of every
# marketing route, captured before the rebrand, so every filename and every
# pixel still showed the previous product. A screenshot directory that large
# is not a portfolio; it is a place stale images go to be forgotten.
#
# Regenerate locally with:  npm run build && npm start   (then) npm run capture
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
portfolio-screenshots/*
!portfolio-screenshots/01-hero.png
!portfolio-screenshots/02-live-rates.png
!portfolio-screenshots/03-feature-account.png
!portfolio-screenshots/04-feature-convert.png
!portfolio-screenshots/05-analytics.png
!portfolio-screenshots/06-iban-validation.png
!portfolio-screenshots/07-mobile.png

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Portfolio video.
#
# scripts/record-demo.mjs writes the raw take here and encodes it alongside.
# None of it is tracked, and that is a deliberate difference from the
# screenshots above: the eight PNGs are embedded in README.md, so the repository
# has to carry them, while the video is uploaded to a listing and read from
# nowhere in this codebase. Twenty-seven megabytes of takes would sit in the
# history of a public repository forever to serve a file nothing here loads.
#
# Regenerate locally with:  npm run build && npm start   (then) npm run record
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
portfolio-video/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Upwork Project Catalog image set.
#
# Same rule, same reasoning. These are eight upload artefacts photographed from
# the deployed site by `npm run capture:catalog`, and three of them overlap with
# the README gallery above — committing both sets would put near-duplicate
# images in the history of a public repository to serve a listing that lives on
# another site entirely.
#
# Regenerate with:  npm run capture:catalog
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
upwork-catalog/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Throwaway scripts.
#
# Puppeteer one-offs written to look at something once — check a nav state,
# crop a box, watch the ticker tick. Nine of them had accumulated in the root,
# untracked but NOT ignored, which on a public repository is one `git add -A`
# away from being published. They are scratch paper: none is referenced by the
# app, by a test, or by package.json, and anything worth keeping belongs in
# scripts/ with a name and a reason.
#
# The convention this encodes: a scratch script is dot-prefixed and lives in
# the root. Everything durable is in scripts/ and is tracked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.*.mjs
!/.eslintrc.mjs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Throwaway artefacts — the output side of the same convention.
#
# The rule above ignores the scratch *scripts*. It did not ignore what they
# write, and that gap is the more dangerous half: `.qa-funnel.tmp.png` sat in
# the root untracked-but-not-ignored, and it is a screenshot of the operator
# funnel. Every rule this project has about images — never photograph
# `/admin`'s submissions view, never publish a frame with a real name in it —
# is enforced in `scripts/`, and none of it reaches a PNG a debugging session
# dropped in the root. One `git add -A` publishes it to a public repository,
# permanently.
#
# So the convention is extended rather than restated: dot-prefixed in the root
# means scratch, whatever the extension. Nothing tracked here is a root
# dot-file of these types — the tracked set is `.env.example`, `.gitignore`,
# and the `.github/`, `.vscode/`, `.data/` directories, none of which match.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.*.png
/.*.jpg
/.*.jpeg
/.*.webp
/.*.mp4
/.*.json
!/.eslintrc.json
